Math Functions in C
What I Learned

Today, I learned about mathematical functions in C programming using the math.h library.
C provides several built-in mathematical functions that help us perform calculations easily without writing complicated formulas manually.
In this program, I practiced using different math functions and storing their results in variables.

Math Functions I Learned
1. sqrt() – Square Root
Used to find the square root of a number.
Example: sqrt(2) returns approximately 1.414.

2. pow() – Power Function
Used to raise a number to a specific power.
Example: pow(2, 3) returns 8.

3. round() – Round Function
Rounds a floating-point number to the nearest integer.
Example: round(3.14) returns 3.

4. ceil() – Ceiling Function
Rounds a number up to the next integer.
Example: ceil(3.14) returns 4.

5. floor() – Floor Function
Rounds a number down to the previous integer.
Example: floor(3.14) returns 3.

6. abs() – Absolute Value
Returns the positive value of an integer, removing its negative sign if present.
Example: abs(-3) returns 3.

7. log() – Logarithmic Function
Calculates the natural logarithm of a number.
Example: log(5) returns approximately 1.609.

8. sin() – Trigonometric Function
Calculates the sine of an angle.
The angle must be given in radians.
Example: sin(5) returns approximately -0.959.

Important Things I Learned

We need to include #include <math.h> to use mathematical functions.
Different functions are used for different mathematical operations.
Variables are used to store the results of calculations.
Some math functions return floating-point values, so using the correct data type is important.
Trigonometric functions such as sin() use radians instead of degrees.
Conclusion

This lesson helped me understand how to use built-in mathematical functions in C. 
I learned how to perform square roots, powers, rounding, absolute value calculations, logarithms, and trigonometric operations.
It also improved my understanding of the math.h library and how mathematical operations can be performed easily in C programming.
